<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  The effects the mod still draws itself: the motor plume, the muzzle flash and the tracers. A
  warhead's burst is KSA's own explosion, from Ksa/Detonation.cs. Modelled on Core's
  ParticleEmitterAssets.xml, which is the only documentation this format has.

  The stages are written out in full in each emitter rather than composed by Id. Core does compose
  that way - Debug_SphericalBurst pulls in Billboard and ThrusterSparks - but doing the same here
  threw "Invalid renderer type" out of ParticleSystem, which is what it says when an emitter in the
  tree has no renderer: the by-Id child did not resolve back to its definition. Inline nesting is
  the form every emitter Core actually uses in play, and it works. The duplication is deliberate.

  The muzzle smoke is Billboard, an alpha-blended camera-facing quad sampling a material. A soft
  sprite does not read as a ball because it has no edge, and tools/model/smokepuff.py makes it.

  Colour components above 1 are deliberate - Core's ThrusterSparks runs at 15/11/6 - because the
  renderer is HDR and that is what makes a flash bloom rather than read as flat orange paint.

  ParticleSpawnLogic is radial, never Fountain. Fountain builds a cone about the emitter's local
  +X, and an effect placed in a body-fixed frame has its model matrix forced to identity - so that
  +X would be a fixed direction in the world and every flash would spray the same way whatever
  fired it. ScatterFromCenter and MoveAwayFromCenter take their direction from each particle's own
  offset from the centre, so they need no orientation to be correct.

  GrowOverLifetime NEEDS EmitterExtra W, and the failure is silent and backwards. The shader is
  mix(initialScale, initialScale * extra.w, lifeRatio^2), so W defaults to zero and the updater
  named "grow" shrinks the particle to nothing on a quadratic curve. Every smoke stage here has a W,
  and a stage that grows without one is imploding.

  Density is set on every stage, and it must be: a stage without one falls at full local gravity.
  That is right for debris and wrong for everything else - a flame that falls looks like the motor
  stopped, and smoke that falls sinks into the ground within a second. KSA scales gravity by 1 - airDensity / Density, so each value is 1.225 / (1 - g) for the
  fraction g of gravity the stage should feel in sea-level air. Thinner air lifts less, and below
  100 Pa KSA counts no air at all: there every stage falls at full gravity whatever it says.

  InheritVelocity is off on the muzzle flash and ON for the motor plume, and the difference is not
  the parent. Muzzle gas is left in the air where it was fired; a plume hangs off something doing
  840 m/s, and without it the particles are left standing in the world while
  the round leaves them 14 m apart. It works from a celestial parent - the flame staying with the
  round rather than streaking behind it is what proved that.
-->
<Assets>
    <!--
      The sprite the billboard smoke is drawn with: white, with alpha carrying a soft radial
      falloff, so ParticleColor alone decides the tint. Generated by tools/model/smokepuff.py and
      checked for reproducibility in CI the same way the palette is.
    -->
    <PbrMaterial Id="KSArmorySmokeMaterial">
        <Diffuse Path="Textures/KSArmory_Smoke_Diffuse.png" Category="Vessel" />
        <Normal Path="Textures/KSArmory_Smoke_Normal.png" Category="Vessel" />
        <AoRoughMetal Path="Textures/KSArmory_Smoke_PBR.png" Category="Vessel" />
    </PbrMaterial>

    <!--
      Rocket motor plume. Held open while the round burns and handed back at burnout, so its spawn
      mode is Endless rather than Burst.

      InheritVelocity is the setting that makes it a plume rather than a string of beads. A round
      at 840 m/s covers 14 m between frames, and particles left at rest in the world would spawn
      as a dotted line the round runs away from; carrying the emitter's velocity, they trail it.
      Core's own ThrusterSparks sets the same flag for the same reason.

      Short-lived and SMALL: this is the flame at a 127 mm nozzle, not a fireball. Core's own
      ThrusterSparks runs particles of 1-3 cm; anything approaching the body's own diameter stops
      reading as exhaust and starts hiding the round, which is what the chase camera looks at.
      A long lifespan on something moving this fast also draws a streak kilometres long.

      Density is sea-level air's, so the flame floats - see the note at the top of this file.
      Exhaust that falls looks like the motor stopped.
    -->
    <ParticleEmitter Id="KSArmoryMotorPlume" Density="1.225">
        <MaxParticles Value="64" />
        <Mesh Id="ParticleSphere"/>
        <Renderer>SimpleColor</Renderer>
        <Updaters>SimpleMovement</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ShrinkOverLifetime</Updaters>
        <Updaters>HueShift</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ModelMatrix</Updaters>
        <Updaters>Age</Updaters>
        <SpawnMode>Endless</SpawnMode>
        <EmitterSpawnLogic>Sphere</EmitterSpawnLogic>
        <EmitterSize Value="0.05"/>
        <EmitterExtents X="0.04" Y="0" Z="0"/>
        <SpawnRate Value="90"/>
        <Force X="0" Y="0" Z="0" />
        <InheritVelocity Value="true"/>
        <ParticleColor X="12" Y="6" Z="2" W="1"/>
        <ParticleColorShift X="0.35" Y="0.2" Z="0"/>
        <ParticleLifespan X="0.05" Y="0.14"/>
        <ParticleSpawnLogic>ScatterFromCenter</ParticleSpawnLogic>
        <ParticleSize X="0.05" Y="0.14"/>
        <ParticleVelocity X="2.5" Y="2.5" Z="2.5"/>
        <ParticleVelocityShift Value="0.7"/>
        <ParticleExtra X="0.5" Y="0" Z="0" W="0"/>
    </ParticleEmitter>

    <!--
      Muzzle flash. Held open while the gun is firing rather than burst per round: a CIWS cycles
      at 75 rounds a second, and taking an emitter from the pool that often would drain it in a
      second and leave nothing in the world able to spawn particles at all.

      InheritVelocity is deliberately OFF, unlike the motor plume. Muzzle gas is left standing in
      the air while the mount moves on, which is what it does in life. The emitter is re-anchored
      to the barrels every frame, so each frame's particles start in the right place regardless.

      Small and very short-lived. This is the flash at a 20 mm muzzle, about a metre of it, and a
      long lifespan at this spawn rate builds a permanent fireball round the barrels instead.
      Colour components above 1 are HDR, which is what makes it bloom rather than read as paint.
    -->
    <ParticleEmitter Id="KSArmoryMuzzleFlash" Density="1.225">
        <MaxParticles Value="64" />
        <Mesh Id="ParticleSphere"/>
        <Renderer>SimpleColor</Renderer>
        <Updaters>SimpleMovement</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ShrinkOverLifetime</Updaters>
        <Updaters>HueShift</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ModelMatrix</Updaters>
        <Updaters>Age</Updaters>
        <SpawnMode>Endless</SpawnMode>
        <EmitterSpawnLogic>Sphere</EmitterSpawnLogic>
        <EmitterSize Value="0.18"/>
        <EmitterExtents X="0.12" Y="0" Z="0"/>
        <SpawnRate Value="220"/>
        <Force X="0" Y="0" Z="0" />
        <InheritVelocity Value="false"/>
        <ParticleColor X="20" Y="11" Z="3" W="1"/>
        <ParticleColorShift X="0.3" Y="0.2" Z="0"/>
        <ParticleLifespan X="0.04" Y="0.11"/>
        <ParticleSpawnLogic>ScatterFromCenter</ParticleSpawnLogic>
        <ParticleSize X="0.22" Y="0.62"/>
        <ParticleVelocity X="7" Y="7" Z="7"/>
        <ParticleVelocityShift Value="0.7"/>
        <ParticleExtra X="0.5" Y="0" Z="0" W="0"/>

        <!-- Propellant smoke, which is what says the gun has been firing rather than just fired. -->
        <ParticleEmitters Density="1.189">
            <MaxParticles Value="96" />
            <Mesh Id="Plane"/>
            <Renderer>Billboard</Renderer>
            <MaterialId Value="KSArmorySmokeMaterial"/>
            <Updaters>SimpleMovement</Updaters>
            <Updaters>GrowOverLifetime</Updaters>
            <!-- W is the grow ratio: without it the curve runs to zero and the smoke implodes. -->
            <EmitterExtra W="2.2"/>
            <Updaters>ModelMatrix</Updaters>
            <Updaters>Age</Updaters>
            <SpawnMode>Endless</SpawnMode>
            <EmitterSpawnLogic>Sphere</EmitterSpawnLogic>
            <EmitterSize Value="0.25"/>
            <EmitterExtents X="0.15" Y="0" Z="0"/>
            <!--
              The smoke is most of what a firing gun looks like, and it wants to accumulate: each
              puff is faint, and a burst piles them into a haze that hangs at the muzzles and
              drifts. A short lifespan gives single puffs that vanish and reads as nothing at all.
            -->
            <SpawnRate Value="55"/>
            <Force X="0" Y="0" Z="0" />
            <InheritVelocity Value="false"/>
            <ParticleColor X="0.36" Y="0.33" Z="0.30" W="0.30"/>
            <ParticleLifespan X="0.9" Y="2.4"/>
            <ParticleSpawnLogic>ScatterFromCenter</ParticleSpawnLogic>
            <ParticleSize X="0.5" Y="1.8"/>
            <ParticleVelocity X="2.0" Y="2.0" Z="2.0"/>
            <ParticleVelocityShift Value="0.9"/>
            <ParticleExtra X="0.8" Y="0" Z="0" W="0"/>
        </ParticleEmitters>
    </ParticleEmitter>

    <!--
      Tracers. One emitter rides a shell and is moved to it every frame, so the particles it leaves
      behind mark the path the round actually took.

      It cannot be done from the muzzle instead, which was tried first: EmitterVelocity is assigned
      by the engine only for a vehicle-parented emitter (ParticleEmitter.UpdateUniforms), so from a
      celestial parent InheritVelocity has nothing to inherit and BubbleOrigin.VelocityBub never
      reaches spawning. Particles launched there simply hang at the barrel. Directional spawning is
      no better: the cone is built about a fixed axis of the body frame, not the turret's.

      So the particles carry no velocity of their own and want none. They are short-lived and
      stationary, and the streak comes entirely from the emitter having moved between frames, which
      at 1100 m/s is 18 m a frame.

      Density is sea-level air's, so the marks float: they show where the shell has been, and a
      mark that falls is a mark in the wrong place.
    -->
    <ParticleEmitter Id="KSArmoryTracer" Density="1.225">
        <MaxParticles Value="64" />
        <Mesh Id="ParticleSphere"/>
        <Renderer>SimpleColor</Renderer>
        <Updaters>SimpleMovement</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ShrinkOverLifetime</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ModelMatrix</Updaters>
        <Updaters>Age</Updaters>
        <SpawnMode>Endless</SpawnMode>
        <EmitterSpawnLogic>Sphere</EmitterSpawnLogic>
        <EmitterSize Value="0.02"/>
        <EmitterExtents X="0.02" Y="0" Z="0"/>

        <!--
          Dense and brief, and both numbers follow from the shell's speed. At 1100 m/s the emitter
          moves 18 m between frames, so 120 spawns a second leaves a particle every 9 m: a string
          of beads rather than a streak, and at any size big enough to see they read as fireballs
          flying in formation. 900 a second puts one every 1.2 m, which merges. The lifespan is
          then the streak's LENGTH divided by that speed: 0.05 s is 55 m of tail.
        -->
        <SpawnRate Value="900"/>
        <ParticleLifespan X="0.035" Y="0.06"/>
        <ParticleSize X="0.10" Y="0.20"/>

        <Force X="0" Y="0" Z="0" />
        <InheritVelocity Value="false"/>
        <ParticleColor X="18" Y="7" Z="1.5" W="1"/>
        <ParticleColorShift X="0.2" Y="0.08" Z="0"/>
        <ParticleSpawnLogic>ScatterFromCenter</ParticleSpawnLogic>
        <ParticleVelocity X="0.8" Y="0.8" Z="0.8"/>
        <ParticleVelocityShift Value="0.5"/>
        <ParticleExtra X="0.6" Y="0" Z="0" W="0"/>
    </ParticleEmitter>

    <!--
      The airless nuclear burst, which is two emitters because there is nothing to draw a mushroom
      cloud with and nothing for one to rise through. Both are Burst: they fire once and hand
      themselves back when their particles die, so nothing has to hold or release them. An Endless
      emitter never completes and must be killed by hand - see Ksa/MuzzleFlash.cs.

      Both are Volumetric, which is the renderer worth using and is NOT gated on an atmosphere:
      ParticleSystem.WriteCommandsColorTranslucent dispatches it beside Billboard behind the
      particles setting alone. Its shader includes AtmosphereLuts.glsl, which looks like a
      dependency and is not one.

      Sizes, speeds and lifespans are placeholders. Ksa/BurstEjecta.cs writes them per burst from
      Sim/AirlessBurst.cs, because they scale with the yield and the ejecta's also with the body's
      gravity. What stays here is the look: the renderer, the envelopes and the colour ramp.

      Drag is ZERO on both. There is no air - that is the whole premise - and KSA's own volumetric
      smoke carries 1.2, which would bleed the throw off against an atmosphere that is not there.
    -->

    <!--
      Thrown ground. Density is rock's, so KSA gives it full gravity and the arc comes back down on
      its own: below 100 Pa the engine counts no air and falls every particle at full local gravity
      whatever this says, so on the bodies this spawns on the value is belt and braces.

      ParticleSpawnLogic is MoveAwayFromCenter, which sends each particle out along its own offset
      from the centre. Fountain would aim them and cannot be used: a body-fixed effect has its model
      matrix forced to identity, so its cone would point one fixed way in the world whatever was hit.
      The throw is therefore a full sphere whose lower half is under the ground, where the depth test
      hides it - which is what leaves a dome standing over the crater.

      128 rather than the 256 a billboard version used. Volumetric compositing takes 16 spheres per
      ray, so past that depth the far side of the dome is simply dropped; fewer and larger particles
      read as more dust here, not less.
    -->
    <ParticleEmitter Id="KSArmoryNuclearEjecta" Drag="0" Density="2500">
        <MaxParticles Value="128" />
        <Mesh Id="ParticleSphere"/>
        <Renderer>Volumetric</Renderer>
        <Updaters>SimpleMovement</Updaters>
        <Updaters>Age</Updaters>
        <Updaters>LifetimeEnvelope</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ModelMatrix</Updaters>
        <SpawnMode>Burst</SpawnMode>
        <EmitterSpawnLogic>Sphere</EmitterSpawnLogic>
        <EmitterSize Value="1"/>
        <!-- On Burst this is the number of particles, not a rate. -->
        <SpawnRate Value="128"/>
        <Force X="0" Y="0" Z="0" />
        <InheritVelocity Value="false"/>
        <Opacity Value="0.85"/>
        <ParticleColor X="0.5" Y="0.45" Z="0.38" W="1"/>
        <ParticleLifespan X="1" Y="2"/>
        <ParticleSpawnLogic>MoveAwayFromCenter</ParticleSpawnLogic>
        <ParticleSize X="1" Y="2"/>
        <ParticleVelocity X="1" Y="1" Z="1"/>
        <!--
          Ragged, but not so wide that the curtain stops being one. At 0.5 the fast quarter left at
          1.5x the speed, which is 2.25x the reach and 2.25x the apex: the dome came apart into a
          few clumps, some of them over the horizon from a camera standing at 98 m on a burst whose
          own apex is 52. AirlessBurst.SpeedSpread carries the same number to the lifespans, which
          have to match it, because a grain's flight is set by the speed it left at: a spread in one
          and not the other strands fast grains in mid-air and buries slow ones.
        -->
        <ParticleVelocityShift Value="0.25"/>
        <!--
          A sprite here is a PACKET of grains, not a grain, and that is what sets the growth. A
          grain in vacuum does not swell - there is no air to entrain, and growing one 3.4x read
          correctly as smoke on an airless body. But a packet of grains leaving with a spread of
          speeds separates LINEARLY with time, and in vacuum nothing ever slows that spread, so the
          packet's own width grows for the whole flight.

          Flat, it came apart: the dome was one mass at 1.6 s and about thirty countable blobs with
          clear ground between them by 4.6 s, because the packets flew apart while each stayed the
          size it left at. Roughly doubling over the flight is what keeps the sheet continuous, and
          it is the same 'no drag' fact that argues against swelling a single grain.

          The alpha holds until the dust lands. Fading it in mid-air is dissipation, the same
          atmospheric mistake in the other channel: vacuum ejecta does not thin out, it arrives.
          At 0.82 a third of the dome had gone before it came down and the last capture of a 16 s
          flight, taken at 12.7 s, was empty ground.

          The colour barely moves for the same reason. Regolith is the same colour at the top of the
          arc as it was leaving the crater, where smoke pales as it dilutes.
        -->
        <ScaleEnvelope Start="1" Peak="1.55" PeakAt="0.5" End="1.95"/>
        <AlphaEnvelope Start="0.95" Peak="0.95" PeakAt="0.94" End="0"/>
        <ColorStops MidAt="0.5">
            <Start R="0.50" G="0.45" B="0.38"/>
            <Mid R="0.48" G="0.43" B="0.37"/>
            <End R="0.45" G="0.41" B="0.35"/>
        </ColorStops>
    </ParticleEmitter>

    <!--
      The debris shell: the bomb's own vaporised mass, which in vacuum keeps going instead of
      settling into a fireball.

      ONE particle, spawned at a Point with no velocity, expanded entirely by its ScaleEnvelope.
      That is Core's ShockWave design and its reasoning holds here: any spawn velocity on a single
      volumetric reads as a flying blob rather than a front, and a spray of them reads as sparks.
      EmitterExtra Y is the Volumetric renderer's emissive term, which is what makes it a glowing
      shell rather than a grey ball.

      Volumetric.comp erodes the boundary by sqrt(1 - t^2), so the visible front peaks near 74% of
      the authored radius and then contracts - hence the End overshoot, so growth outruns erosion.
    -->
    <ParticleEmitter Id="KSArmoryNuclearShell" Drag="0" Density="1.225">
        <MaxParticles Value="1" />
        <Mesh Id="ParticleSphere"/>
        <Renderer>Volumetric</Renderer>
        <Updaters>SimpleMovement</Updaters>
        <Updaters>Age</Updaters>
        <Updaters>LifetimeEnvelope</Updaters>
        <Updaters>ModelMatrix</Updaters>
        <SpawnMode>Burst</SpawnMode>
        <EmitterSpawnLogic>Point</EmitterSpawnLogic>
        <EmitterSize Value="1"/>
        <SpawnRate Value="1"/>
        <Force X="0" Y="0" Z="0" />
        <InheritVelocity Value="false"/>
        <!-- Y is emissive brightness: bright, because this one IS the fireball's replacement. -->
        <EmitterExtra X="0" Y="2.5" Z="0" W="0"/>
        <Opacity Value="0.6"/>
        <ParticleColor X="18" Y="11" Z="5" W="1"/>
        <ParticleLifespan X="1" Y="2"/>
        <ParticleSpawnLogic>Basic</ParticleSpawnLogic>
        <ParticleSize X="1" Y="2"/>
        <ParticleVelocity X="0" Y="0" Z="0"/>
        <ParticleVelocityShift Value="0"/>
        <ScaleEnvelope Start="0.08" Peak="0.9" PeakAt="0.2" End="1.5"/>
        <AlphaEnvelope Start="0.95" Peak="0.5" PeakAt="0.3" End="0"/>
        <!--
          Cools to nothing rather than to grey. Ending on a dull grey is a smoke colour and leaves a
          puff hanging where the shell was; this is bomb vapour thinning out, so it goes dark.
        -->
        <ColorStops MidAt="0.3">
            <Start R="18" G="11" B="5"/>
            <Mid R="4" G="1.6" B="0.5"/>
            <End R="0.15" G="0.10" B="0.08"/>
        </ColorStops>
    </ParticleEmitter>

    <!--
      The burst a nuclear charge goes off as. Core's Explosion_Conflagration with its SmokeVolume
      left out, and nothing else changed.

      That volume is the reason a mushroom cloud stood over a bright pale-blue lump: it is sized for
      a conventional blast, it is drawn at the burst point, and its own ColorStops end on 0.6/0.6/0.65
      - so it sits under a 1.3 km column of dirty smoke looking like a different weather system.
      Tinting and thinning this mod's own pens could never reach it, because it is not this mod's.

      Everything a nuclear burst does want is kept: the flash, the fireball volume, the shockwave and
      the bang. What replaces the smoke is Sim/MushroomCloud.cs, which is drawing the same air at a
      thousand times the size.

      The debris emitters go with it. They throw metal, which is right for a vehicle coming apart and
      wrong for a device going off over ground; Ksa/BurstEjecta.cs throws what a surface burst throws,
      on the bodies where it can be seen.
    -->
    <Explosion Id="KSArmoryNuclearBurst">
        <Flash PeakIntensity="5000" Range="200" DecaySeconds="0.2" LifeSeconds="0.55" ColorMidAt="0.3">
            <ColorStart R="1" G="0.97" B="0.9"/>
            <ColorMid R="1" G="0.72" B="0.28"/>
            <ColorEnd R="1" G="0.25" B="0.05"/>
        </Flash>
        <SoundEvent SoundId="ExplosionBig"/>
        <VolumeRef Id="FireballVolume" Delay="0" Intensity="1.2" FallbackEmitterId="FireballVolumetric"/>
        <VolumeRef Id="ShockWaveVolume" Delay="0" Intensity="1.0" FallbackEmitterId="ShockWave"/>
    </Explosion>

    <!--
      The mod's own compute shader, declared exactly as Core declares its own: KSA compiles a
      Shader asset from any mod's folder, so nothing has to be ported to get one onto the GPU.
      Ksa/CloudPass.cs dispatches it.
    -->
    <Shader Id="KSArmoryCloudCompute" Path="Shaders/KSArmoryCloud.comp" />

</Assets>
